example.code:
var context = new HttpAssertContext
{
    Client = httpClient,
    Url = "/api/users",
    Parameters = new[] { ("{userId}", 123) },
    WriteResponse = true
};

await client.AssertGetAsync(context);

var context = new HttpAssertContext<User>
{
    Client = httpClient,
    Url = "/api/users/{userId}",
    ExpectedResult = "expected-user.json",
    Parameters = new[] { ("{userId}", 123) },
    FilterFunc = user => user with { Id = default },
    WriteResponse = true
};

var user = await HttpClientAssertExtensions.AssertGetAsync(context);

member.summary:
A short, human-readable summary of the problem type. It SHOULD NOT change from occurrence to occurrence
of the problem, except for purposes of localization(e.g., using proactive content negotiation;
see[RFC7231], Section 3.4).

member.example:
Applying the attribute without a path or additional parameters:
[RequestLocator] public void TestSomeUseCase(...) { }
In this scenario, the attribute automatically calculates the request folder from the test class's namespace and supplies just the JSON identifier for each test case.
Applying the attribute with an explicit folder and additional parameters:
[RequestLocator("Api.User.V1.Create.Status_200_Ok.Requests", "ExtraParam", 42)] public void TestSomeUseCase(...) { }
Here, the test case data will include the JSON identifier followed by the extra parameters "ExtraParam" and 42.

